            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for an informer, then the Latin for a defence."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: condemnō, condemnāre",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "condemnō, condemnāre — \"to condemn, to find guilty\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Iūdicēs eum furtī condemnāvērunt. The judges found him guilty of theft."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "condemnō, condemnāre, condemnāvī, condemnātum, with the charge in the genitive. English condemn."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "condemnō is built from con- and damnō \"to condemn\" (EtymDB; Lewis & Short), which comes from damnum \"a loss, a fine\". English condemn comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
